1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,096
60 to 80 million people.

2
00:00:02,096 --> 00:00:03,638
Dead in just 6 years.

3
00:00:03,638 --> 00:00:07,309
History had never counted losses like this before.

4
00:00:07,309 --> 00:00:09,659
It has never counted them since.

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,201
Every continent.

6
00:00:12,201 --> 00:00:13,252
Every ocean.

7
00:00:13,252 --> 00:00:14,128
Every sky.

8
00:00:14,128 --> 00:00:18,506
This war was fought everywhere humans could reach.

9
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:26,124
And 2.5 million Indian soldiers fought in it — the largest volunteer army in history.

10
00:00:26,124 --> 00:00:28,567
A story most documentaries skip.

11
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,939
And yet, most of us were never told this story whole.

12
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:38,581
Tonight, in one sitting — the complete story of the Second World War.

13
00:00:38,581 --> 00:00:41,329
From the first shot to the last surrender.

14
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:43,386
Who fought.

15
00:00:43,386 --> 00:00:44,724
Why they fought.

16
00:00:44,724 --> 00:00:45,977
How it was won.

17
00:00:45,977 --> 00:00:49,572
And what India gave — and what it got back.

18
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:53,145
Because this is not just history.

19
00:00:53,145 --> 00:00:54,331
It is a warning.

20
00:00:55,467 --> 00:00:58,507
This is the whole story of the Second World War.

21
00:00:58,507 --> 00:01:00,535
From the first shot to the last.

22
00:01:01,667 --> 00:01:02,746
Chapter one.

23
00:01:02,746 --> 00:01:04,096
The powder keg.

24
00:01:05,233 --> 00:01:11,411
After the First World War, the Treaty of Versailles crushed Germany.

25
00:01:11,411 --> 00:01:11,956
Blame.

26
00:01:11,956 --> 00:01:12,410
Debt.

27
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:13,501
Humiliation.

28
00:01:13,501 --> 00:01:15,499
All of it, in writing.

29
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:18,729
Then came economic ruin.

30
00:01:18,729 --> 00:01:21,086
The hyperinflation of 1923.

31
00:01:21,086 --> 00:01:23,618
The Great Depression of 1929.

32
00:01:23,618 --> 00:01:26,586
Money and hope collapsed together.

33
00:01:27,733 --> 00:01:31,095
A broken, angry people wanted a savior.

34
00:01:31,095 --> 00:01:33,767
What they got was Adolf Hitler.

35
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:37,367
In 1933 he became chancellor.

36
00:01:37,367 --> 00:01:40,856
Within months, German democracy was dead.

37
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,096
He rebuilt the army in secret.

38
00:01:44,096 --> 00:01:47,799
Then he started swallowing land, one piece at a time.

39
00:01:48,933 --> 00:01:50,367
The Rhineland.

40
00:01:50,367 --> 00:01:51,186
Austria.

41
00:01:51,186 --> 00:01:52,721
Czechoslovakia.

42
00:01:53,867 --> 00:01:55,743
Britain and France watched.

43
00:01:55,743 --> 00:01:58,800
To avoid a war, they negotiated with Hitler.

44
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:00,398
They never stopped him.

45
00:02:01,533 --> 00:02:03,441
History calls it appeasement.

46
00:02:03,441 --> 00:02:05,086
And it failed completely.

47
00:02:06,233 --> 00:02:07,129
August 1939.

48
00:02:07,129 --> 00:02:13,103
Hitler and Stalin signed a secret pact — a deal to carve up Poland between them.

49
00:02:14,233 --> 00:02:16,349
Now all it needed was a spark.

50
00:02:17,467 --> 00:02:18,611
Chapter two.

51
00:02:18,611 --> 00:02:19,661
Blitzkrieg.

52
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:22,480
September 1, 1939.

53
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:23,600
Before dawn.

54
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:27,801
German tanks rolled across the Polish border.

55
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:32,004
This was an entirely new kind of war.

56
00:02:32,004 --> 00:02:32,917
Blitzkrieg.

57
00:02:32,917 --> 00:02:34,079
Lightning war.

58
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:37,535
First the bombers cleared the way.

59
00:02:37,535 --> 00:02:40,282
Then the tanks punched through at speed.

60
00:02:40,282 --> 00:02:43,167
The infantry followed, and held what fell.

61
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:45,916
Poland fought bravely.

62
00:02:45,916 --> 00:02:49,368
But horses and old artillery do not stop tanks.

63
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:54,627
On September 3, Britain and France declared war on Germany.

64
00:02:55,767 --> 00:02:57,644
But no help ever arrived.

65
00:02:57,644 --> 00:03:03,578
In 5 weeks, Poland was gone — Germany from the west, the Soviets from the east.

66
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:06,759
Then came 8 strange months.

67
00:03:06,759 --> 00:03:09,961
War was declared, but nobody was fighting.

68
00:03:09,961 --> 00:03:12,249
They called it the Phoney War.

69
00:03:13,367 --> 00:03:16,145
In April 1940, the storm returned.

70
00:03:16,145 --> 00:03:19,088
Denmark and Norway fell within days.

71
00:03:20,233 --> 00:03:22,945
In May, Hitler struck west.

72
00:03:22,945 --> 00:03:23,748
Holland.

73
00:03:23,748 --> 00:03:24,551
Belgium.

74
00:03:24,551 --> 00:03:27,965
And then the real target — France.

75
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:35,631
France had the largest army in Europe, and the Maginot Line — a wall of fortresses considered impossible to break.

76
00:03:36,767 --> 00:03:39,250
The German tanks never fought that wall.

77
00:03:39,250 --> 00:03:43,533
They swung through the dense Ardennes forest, and came out behind it.

78
00:03:44,667 --> 00:03:49,683
The British and French armies were trapped against the sea — on the beach at Dunkirk.

79
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:53,232
And then, something like a miracle.

80
00:03:53,232 --> 00:04:01,641
Alongside the navy came ordinary civilians, in their own small boats, sailing into a war zone to bring the soldiers home.

81
00:04:02,767 --> 00:04:06,973
In 9 days, 338,000 soldiers were rescued.

82
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:11,114
But France could not be saved.

83
00:04:11,114 --> 00:04:14,631
June 22, 1940 — France surrendered.

84
00:04:15,767 --> 00:04:22,517
And Hitler staged the signing in the same railway carriage where Germany had surrendered in 1918.

85
00:04:22,517 --> 00:04:23,839
Revenge — complete.

86
00:04:24,967 --> 00:04:25,673
10 months.

87
00:04:25,673 --> 00:04:29,486
And nearly all of Western Europe lay at Hitler's feet.

88
00:04:30,633 --> 00:04:33,713
Now only one country stood against him.

89
00:04:33,713 --> 00:04:34,186
Alone.

90
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:36,509
Chapter three.

91
00:04:36,509 --> 00:04:37,684
Britain alone.

92
00:04:38,833 --> 00:04:39,654
June 1940.

93
00:04:39,654 --> 00:04:41,131
France had fallen.

94
00:04:41,131 --> 00:04:42,772
America was neutral.

95
00:04:42,772 --> 00:04:46,957
The Soviet Union was still in its pact with Hitler.

96
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:50,919
On the map, one enemy remained — Britain.

97
00:04:50,919 --> 00:04:54,082
And Hitler was certain it would come to terms.

98
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:58,104
But there was a new prime minister in London now.

99
00:04:58,104 --> 00:04:59,171
Winston Churchill.

100
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:02,003
Churchill made it plain.

101
00:05:02,003 --> 00:05:03,635
There would be no deal.

102
00:05:03,635 --> 00:05:05,054
No matter what came.

103
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:09,573
In his own words — we shall fight on the beaches.

104
00:05:09,573 --> 00:05:11,294
We shall never surrender.

105
00:05:12,433 --> 00:05:16,956
Hitler had one option left: cross the English Channel and invade.

106
00:05:16,956 --> 00:05:19,669
The plan was called Operation Sea Lion.

107
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:24,274
But to cross the sea, he first had to win the sky.

108
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:29,781
And so began the first battle in history fought entirely in the air.

109
00:05:29,781 --> 00:05:31,199
The Battle of Britain.

110
00:05:32,333 --> 00:05:37,192
On one side, Germany's Luftwaffe — vastly superior in numbers.

111
00:05:38,333 --> 00:05:41,261
On the other, the Royal Air Force.

112
00:05:41,261 --> 00:05:43,586
Fewer pilots, fewer planes.

113
00:05:43,586 --> 00:05:46,771
But two weapons Germany did not have.

114
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:51,417
The first: the Spitfire and the Hurricane.

115
00:05:51,417 --> 00:05:54,013
Agile, lethal fighter aircraft.

116
00:05:55,133 --> 00:05:58,492
The second: a new secret technology called radar.

117
00:05:58,492 --> 00:06:02,055
German planes were spotted the moment they took off.

118
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:06,023
All summer, the battle raged overhead.

119
00:06:06,023 --> 00:06:08,921
Hundreds of aircraft, every single day.

120
00:06:10,067 --> 00:06:12,852
British pilots flew 4 sorties a day.

121
00:06:12,852 --> 00:06:16,101
Many of them were only 19 or 20 years old.

122
00:06:17,233 --> 00:06:19,414
Then Hitler changed strategy.

123
00:06:19,414 --> 00:06:21,370
The targets became cities.

124
00:06:21,370 --> 00:06:24,679
London was bombed for 57 consecutive nights.

125
00:06:24,679 --> 00:06:26,559
They called it the Blitz.

126
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:28,774
Thousands died.

127
00:06:28,774 --> 00:06:30,636
But Britain did not break.

128
00:06:30,636 --> 00:06:36,294
People slept in Underground stations at night, and went to work in the morning.

129
00:06:37,433 --> 00:06:38,676
September 1940.

130
00:06:38,676 --> 00:06:42,073
Hitler postponed the invasion of Britain.

131
00:06:42,073 --> 00:06:43,232
It never came.

132
00:06:44,367 --> 00:06:46,918
For the first time, the world saw it.

133
00:06:46,918 --> 00:06:48,573
Hitler could be stopped.

134
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:52,456
Churchill made those pilots immortal.

135
00:06:52,456 --> 00:06:55,734
Never was so much owed by so many to so few.

136
00:06:56,867 --> 00:06:58,015
Chapter four.

137
00:06:58,015 --> 00:06:59,871
The greatest mistake.

138
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,232
Blocked at Britain, Hitler turned the map around — and looked east.

139
00:07:06,367 --> 00:07:07,355
The Soviet Union.

140
00:07:07,355 --> 00:07:10,494
The same country he had signed a friendship pact with.

141
00:07:11,633 --> 00:07:15,063
For Hitler, that pact was only ever a trick.

142
00:07:15,063 --> 00:07:16,466
A way to buy time.

143
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:22,589
What he wanted was Soviet land, Ukraine's grain, and the oil of the Caucasus.

144
00:07:23,733 --> 00:07:25,106
June 22, 1941.

145
00:07:25,106 --> 00:07:27,165
Operation Barbarossa.

146
00:07:27,165 --> 00:07:30,890
The largest invasion in human history.

147
00:07:32,033 --> 00:07:33,872
3.8 million soldiers.

148
00:07:33,872 --> 00:07:36,673
Thousands of tanks and aircraft.

149
00:07:36,673 --> 00:07:40,000
Across the border in a single morning.

150
00:07:41,133 --> 00:07:43,131
Stalin refused to believe it.

151
00:07:43,131 --> 00:07:47,402
The warnings kept coming, and he kept dismissing them as lies.

152
00:07:48,533 --> 00:07:51,258
At first, Germany looked unstoppable.

153
00:07:51,258 --> 00:07:55,455
Within weeks, millions of Soviet soldiers were prisoners.

154
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:58,661
But this war was different.

155
00:07:58,661 --> 00:08:00,799
It was not a war to conquer.

156
00:08:00,799 --> 00:08:02,478
It was a war to erase.

157
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:08,786
As the Soviets retreated, they burned everything behind them.

158
00:08:08,786 --> 00:08:09,381
Fields.

159
00:08:09,381 --> 00:08:10,061
Bridges.

160
00:08:10,061 --> 00:08:10,911
Factories.

161
00:08:10,911 --> 00:08:13,971
Nothing would be left for the enemy.

162
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:19,227
Even so, the German army reached within a few kilometers of Moscow.

163
00:08:20,367 --> 00:08:23,891
And then Russia's oldest weapon took the field.

164
00:08:23,891 --> 00:08:24,416
Winter.

165
00:08:25,533 --> 00:08:26,286
Minus 30.

166
00:08:26,286 --> 00:08:27,707
Minus 40 degrees.

167
00:08:27,707 --> 00:08:32,220
The German soldiers did not even have winter clothing.

168
00:08:33,367 --> 00:08:35,148
Tank oil froze solid.

169
00:08:35,148 --> 00:08:37,014
Rifles stopped firing.

170
00:08:37,014 --> 00:08:40,916
Thousands of soldiers died of the cold itself.

171
00:08:42,033 --> 00:08:43,047
December 1941.

172
00:08:43,047 --> 00:08:45,942
The Soviet counterattack outside Moscow.

173
00:08:45,942 --> 00:08:49,922
For the first time in the war, Germany was pushed back.

174
00:08:51,067 --> 00:08:53,566
Hitler was now trapped in a two-front war.

175
00:08:53,566 --> 00:08:57,493
The exact mistake that had drowned Germany in the First World War.

176
00:08:58,633 --> 00:09:03,466
And that very week, on the other side of the planet, another storm broke.

177
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:05,693
Chapter five.

178
00:09:05,693 --> 00:09:07,291
The sleeping giant.

179
00:09:08,433 --> 00:09:11,705
In Asia, another empire was spreading.

180
00:09:11,705 --> 00:09:12,221
Japan.

181
00:09:13,367 --> 00:09:15,301
First the invasion of China.

182
00:09:15,301 --> 00:09:18,618
Then its eyes turned to the entire Asia-Pacific.

183
00:09:19,767 --> 00:09:23,535
In response, America cut Japan's lifeline.

184
00:09:23,535 --> 00:09:23,894
Oil.

185
00:09:25,033 --> 00:09:26,845
Japan had two options.

186
00:09:26,845 --> 00:09:29,892
Back down — or strike America itself.

187
00:09:31,033 --> 00:09:32,563
It chose to strike.

188
00:09:32,563 --> 00:09:36,911
The plan: destroy the American fleet in a single blow.

189
00:09:38,033 --> 00:09:39,802
December 7, 1941.

190
00:09:39,802 --> 00:09:42,196
A quiet Sunday morning.

191
00:09:42,196 --> 00:09:44,381
Pearl Harbor, Hawaii.

192
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:48,543
More than 350 Japanese aircraft.

193
00:09:48,543 --> 00:09:50,254
No warning at all.

194
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:55,416
In 2 hours, 8 battleships were sunk or crippled.

195
00:09:55,416 --> 00:09:57,591
2,400 Americans were dead.

196
00:09:58,733 --> 00:10:01,737
President Roosevelt called it a date which will live in infamy.

197
00:10:02,867 --> 00:10:05,871
The very next day, America entered the war.

198
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,227
And Hitler declared war on America himself.

199
00:10:10,227 --> 00:10:12,329
His second greatest mistake.

200
00:10:13,467 --> 00:10:19,057
Because now the largest factory on Earth — the United States — was on the Allied side.

201
00:10:20,200 --> 00:10:22,619
The war was now truly global.

202
00:10:22,619 --> 00:10:24,954
The Axis against the Allies.

203
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:27,168
Chapter six.

204
00:10:27,168 --> 00:10:28,947
The darkest chapter.

205
00:10:30,067 --> 00:10:33,160
Behind this war, another crime was underway.

206
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:36,676
Quiet, deliberate, and planned to the last detail.

207
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:41,523
Nazi ideology was built on a foundation of hatred.

208
00:10:41,523 --> 00:10:43,756
Above all, hatred of the Jews.

209
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:46,729
First came the laws.

210
00:10:46,729 --> 00:10:48,557
Citizenship revoked.

211
00:10:48,557 --> 00:10:49,563
Jobs taken.

212
00:10:49,563 --> 00:10:53,494
A yellow star stitched onto every identity.

213
00:10:54,633 --> 00:10:56,433
Then came the ghettos.

214
00:10:56,433 --> 00:10:59,625
Walled-off districts inside the cities.

215
00:10:59,625 --> 00:11:00,197
Sealed.

216
00:11:01,333 --> 00:11:07,132
And then, in 1942, the Nazi leadership approved what they called the Final Solution.

217
00:11:08,267 --> 00:11:12,456
Trains began running from every corner of Europe.

218
00:11:12,456 --> 00:11:15,021
Destination — the death camps.

219
00:11:15,021 --> 00:11:15,876
Auschwitz.

220
00:11:15,876 --> 00:11:16,731
Treblinka.

221
00:11:17,867 --> 00:11:22,151
What happened there was the most organized crime in human history.

222
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:25,416
6 million Jews were murdered.

223
00:11:26,533 --> 00:11:29,294
And millions more alongside them.

224
00:11:29,294 --> 00:11:29,712
Roma.

225
00:11:29,712 --> 00:11:30,799
The disabled.

226
00:11:30,799 --> 00:11:32,221
Prisoners of war.

227
00:11:32,221 --> 00:11:34,814
Every voice that was different.

228
00:11:35,933 --> 00:11:37,801
History gave it a name.

229
00:11:37,801 --> 00:11:38,937
The Holocaust.

230
00:11:40,067 --> 00:11:42,888
These are not just numbers.

231
00:11:42,888 --> 00:11:45,396
Every number was a name.

232
00:11:45,396 --> 00:11:46,127
A face.

233
00:11:46,127 --> 00:11:47,068
A family.

234
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:50,955
Afterward, the world made a vow.

235
00:11:50,955 --> 00:11:51,988
Never again.

236
00:11:53,133 --> 00:11:55,323
Remembering is the first duty.

237
00:11:55,323 --> 00:11:57,731
Because those who forget, repeat.

238
00:11:58,867 --> 00:11:59,895
Chapter seven.

239
00:11:59,895 --> 00:12:01,218
War in the desert.

240
00:12:02,367 --> 00:12:06,462
Thousands of kilometers from Europe, another front was burning.

241
00:12:06,462 --> 00:12:08,088
The North African desert.

242
00:12:09,233 --> 00:12:14,823
At stake: the Suez Canal — the lifeline of the British Empire — and the oil of the Middle East.

243
00:12:15,967 --> 00:12:17,814
Italy went in first.

244
00:12:17,814 --> 00:12:19,755
And was beaten badly.

245
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:24,010
So Hitler sent his most cunning general.

246
00:12:24,010 --> 00:12:25,021
Erwin Rommel.

247
00:12:25,021 --> 00:12:27,431
They called him the Desert Fox.

248
00:12:28,567 --> 00:12:32,355
Rommel's Afrika Korps drove the British army all the way back toward the sea.

249
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:37,366
And here, in this desert, India's story joined the war.

250
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:41,182
The 4th and 5th Indian Divisions.

251
00:12:41,182 --> 00:12:47,277
Men from Punjab, Maharashtra, Madras, Nepal — holding the line in the sand.

252
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:49,887
The siege of Tobruk.

253
00:12:49,887 --> 00:12:50,854
Sidi Barrani.

254
00:12:50,854 --> 00:12:55,166
In every major battle, Indian regiments were at the front.

255
00:12:56,300 --> 00:12:58,753
Then came the deciding ground.

256
00:12:58,753 --> 00:12:59,652
El Alamein.

257
00:12:59,652 --> 00:13:01,942
The last gateway into Egypt.

258
00:13:03,067 --> 00:13:04,239
October 1942.

259
00:13:04,239 --> 00:13:07,397
General Montgomery's counterattack.

260
00:13:07,397 --> 00:13:09,833
12 days of brutal fighting.

261
00:13:10,967 --> 00:13:11,985
Rommel broke.

262
00:13:11,985 --> 00:13:15,747
The Afrika Korps fell back — and never returned.

263
00:13:16,867 --> 00:13:19,839
Churchill said: this is not the end.

264
00:13:19,839 --> 00:13:23,554
But it is, perhaps, the end of the beginning.

265
00:13:24,700 --> 00:13:29,141
And among the men who paid for that victory, thousands of the names were Indian.

266
00:13:30,267 --> 00:13:31,295
Chapter eight.

267
00:13:31,295 --> 00:13:32,618
India and the war.

268
00:13:33,767 --> 00:13:37,417
Now the story that most history books give a single page.

269
00:13:37,417 --> 00:13:39,723
Most documentaries skip it entirely.

270
00:13:40,867 --> 00:13:46,353
In 1939, Viceroy Linlithgow made an announcement: India is at war.

271
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:50,563
Not a single Indian leader was consulted.

272
00:13:50,563 --> 00:13:54,971
The fate of 300 million people, decided with one signature.

273
00:13:56,100 --> 00:13:58,589
Congress resigned in protest.

274
00:13:58,589 --> 00:14:00,306
The country seethed.

275
00:14:01,433 --> 00:14:05,914
And yet — 2.5 million Indians put on the uniform.

276
00:14:05,914 --> 00:14:07,376
No conscription.

277
00:14:07,376 --> 00:14:08,199
No force.

278
00:14:08,199 --> 00:14:10,942
Every one of them chose to go.

279
00:14:12,067 --> 00:14:14,418
The largest volunteer army in history.

280
00:14:15,567 --> 00:14:17,396
They fought everywhere.

281
00:14:17,396 --> 00:14:19,146
The deserts of Africa.

282
00:14:19,146 --> 00:14:20,975
The mountains of Italy.

283
00:14:20,975 --> 00:14:22,646
The jungles of Burma.

284
00:14:23,767 --> 00:14:26,748
Indians on the heights of Monte Cassino.

285
00:14:26,748 --> 00:14:29,432
Indians in the streets of Singapore.

286
00:14:29,432 --> 00:14:32,936
Iraq, Iran, Egypt — on every front of this war.

287
00:14:34,067 --> 00:14:40,937
More than 30 Victoria Crosses — the highest award for valor — were pinned on Indian chests.

288
00:14:42,067 --> 00:14:44,653
But back home, the story was dark.

289
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:46,244
1943.

290
00:14:46,244 --> 00:14:46,866
Bengal.

291
00:14:46,866 --> 00:14:53,532
The harvests existed — but the rice was leaving, requisitioned for the war.

292
00:14:54,667 --> 00:14:56,055
Prices exploded.

293
00:14:56,055 --> 00:14:58,310
The granaries stood empty.

294
00:14:58,310 --> 00:15:01,433
And the help came far, far too late.

295
00:15:02,567 --> 00:15:06,736
The estimates say 2 to 3 million people starved to death.

296
00:15:06,736 --> 00:15:08,053
The Bengal famine.

297
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:13,253
Historians still argue whether it was negligence or policy.

298
00:15:13,253 --> 00:15:14,764
The dead do not argue.

299
00:15:15,900 --> 00:15:19,645
And in these same years, another path was chosen.

300
00:15:19,645 --> 00:15:22,091
The path of Subhas Chandra Bose.

301
00:15:23,233 --> 00:15:26,629
Bose believed the enemy of the British could be a friend of India.

302
00:15:27,767 --> 00:15:32,178
With Japan's backing, he raised the Indian National Army.

303
00:15:32,178 --> 00:15:33,958
Around 40,000 soldiers.

304
00:15:35,100 --> 00:15:39,854
His call echoed across Asia: give me blood, and I will give you freedom.

305
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:41,384
1944.

306
00:15:41,384 --> 00:15:47,687
The Japanese army and the Indian National Army reached the border of India itself.

307
00:15:48,833 --> 00:15:50,146
Kohima and Imphal.

308
00:15:50,146 --> 00:15:52,626
The hills of Nagaland and Manipur.

309
00:15:52,626 --> 00:15:55,834
History calls it the Stalingrad of the East.

310
00:15:56,967 --> 00:16:00,379
Trenches faced each other across the width of a tennis court.

311
00:16:00,379 --> 00:16:02,113
Weeks of hand-to-hand fighting.

312
00:16:03,233 --> 00:16:05,605
And who stopped Japan there?

313
00:16:05,605 --> 00:16:08,484
In huge numbers — Indian soldiers.

314
00:16:09,633 --> 00:16:12,611
Indians, on both sides of the same battle.

315
00:16:12,611 --> 00:16:15,589
One of the strangest tragedies in history.

316
00:16:16,733 --> 00:16:20,971
That defeat was Japan's largest on land, anywhere in the war.

317
00:16:20,971 --> 00:16:22,846
It happened on Indian soil.

318
00:16:23,967 --> 00:16:26,475
When the war ended, India had changed.

319
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:31,649
Hands that had learned to fight across the world would not accept chains again.

320
00:16:32,767 --> 00:16:36,171
The Indian National Army trials united the country.

321
00:16:36,171 --> 00:16:38,174
Then the navy itself mutinied.

322
00:16:39,300 --> 00:16:40,936
The British understood.

323
00:16:40,936 --> 00:16:43,070
The army was no longer theirs.

324
00:16:43,070 --> 00:16:45,987
And without the army, there is no empire.

325
00:16:47,133 --> 00:16:51,600
Just two years later — August 15, 1947.

326
00:16:52,733 --> 00:16:56,129
This war is the hidden chapter of India's freedom story.

327
00:16:57,267 --> 00:16:58,540
Chapter nine.

328
00:16:58,540 --> 00:16:59,618
Stalingrad.

329
00:17:00,767 --> 00:17:01,801
Summer, 1942.

330
00:17:01,801 --> 00:17:06,018
Hitler had learned nothing from the defeat at Moscow.

331
00:17:07,167 --> 00:17:11,574
This time the target was the south — the oil of the Caucasus.

332
00:17:11,574 --> 00:17:13,959
And on the way, there was a city.

333
00:17:15,100 --> 00:17:15,858
Stalingrad.

334
00:17:15,858 --> 00:17:18,339
The city that carried Stalin's name.

335
00:17:19,467 --> 00:17:22,580
For Hitler, this was no longer strategy.

336
00:17:22,580 --> 00:17:23,903
It was obsession.

337
00:17:23,903 --> 00:17:27,016
A war between the egos of two dictators.

338
00:17:28,133 --> 00:17:32,260
Stalin's Order Number 227: not one step back.

339
00:17:33,400 --> 00:17:34,335
August 1942.

340
00:17:34,335 --> 00:17:38,154
German bombing reduced the entire city to rubble.

341
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:42,853
But that same rubble became a fortress for the Soviet defenders.

342
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,676
Now the war was in the streets.

343
00:17:46,676 --> 00:17:47,971
House by house.

344
00:17:47,971 --> 00:17:49,093
Room by room.

345
00:17:49,093 --> 00:17:51,079
Staircase by staircase.

346
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:55,220
The German soldiers had a name for it.

347
00:17:55,220 --> 00:17:56,174
Rattenkrieg.

348
00:17:56,174 --> 00:17:57,764
The war of the rats.

349
00:17:58,900 --> 00:18:01,695
Weeks of fighting for a single building.

350
00:18:01,695 --> 00:18:04,699
The railway station changed hands 14 times.

351
00:18:05,833 --> 00:18:06,864
Pavlov's House.

352
00:18:06,864 --> 00:18:11,946
One sergeant and a handful of soldiers held a single building for 58 days.

353
00:18:13,067 --> 00:18:15,376
The snipers ruled the ruins.

354
00:18:15,376 --> 00:18:17,273
Every window was death.

355
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:23,990
Vasily Zaitsev — a shepherd's son — 225 German soldiers.

356
00:18:25,133 --> 00:18:26,841
Then winter returned.

357
00:18:26,841 --> 00:18:28,955
The Volga began to freeze.

358
00:18:28,955 --> 00:18:32,369
And the German supply lines began to snap.

359
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:36,996
And Soviet general Zhukov sprang his trap.

360
00:18:36,996 --> 00:18:38,411
Operation Uranus.

361
00:18:39,533 --> 00:18:40,650
November 1942.

362
00:18:40,650 --> 00:18:45,515
Two Soviet armies swung wide around both sides of the city...

363
00:18:46,633 --> 00:18:49,983
And sealed the German Sixth Army inside.

364
00:18:49,983 --> 00:18:52,243
More than 250,000 soldiers.

365
00:18:52,243 --> 00:18:53,164
Surrounded.

366
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:57,351
Hitler's order: retreat is forbidden.

367
00:18:57,351 --> 00:18:58,506
Keep fighting.

368
00:18:59,633 --> 00:19:02,437
Göring promised to supply them from the air.

369
00:19:02,437 --> 00:19:03,839
The promise was a lie.

370
00:19:04,967 --> 00:19:08,477
The encircled men began to starve.

371
00:19:08,477 --> 00:19:09,716
At minus 30.

372
00:19:09,716 --> 00:19:10,955
Eating rats.

373
00:19:10,955 --> 00:19:11,678
Horses.

374
00:19:11,678 --> 00:19:13,640
Whatever there was.

375
00:19:14,767 --> 00:19:16,712
January 31, 1943.

376
00:19:16,712 --> 00:19:20,488
Field Marshal Paulus surrendered.

377
00:19:21,633 --> 00:19:25,343
Hitler had promoted him to field marshal only the day before.

378
00:19:25,343 --> 00:19:28,869
Because no German field marshal had ever been taken alive.

379
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:33,330
The message was clear: take your own life.

380
00:19:33,330 --> 00:19:34,519
Paulus refused.

381
00:19:35,667 --> 00:19:36,509
The cost?

382
00:19:36,509 --> 00:19:40,719
Around 2 million people, both sides combined.

383
00:19:40,719 --> 00:19:41,936
For one city.

384
00:19:43,067 --> 00:19:45,653
The bloodiest battlefield in human history.

385
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:48,600
And here, the war turned.

386
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:52,991
From this point on, the German army would only move backward.

387
00:19:54,133 --> 00:19:55,622
All the way to Berlin.

388
00:19:56,767 --> 00:19:57,690
Chapter ten.

389
00:19:57,690 --> 00:19:59,614
The Pacific strikes back.

390
00:20:00,733 --> 00:20:03,815
Out in the Pacific, Japan looked unbeatable.

391
00:20:04,933 --> 00:20:10,971
In the 6 months after Pearl Harbor — the Philippines, Malaya, Singapore, Burma.

392
00:20:10,971 --> 00:20:12,117
All of it fell.

393
00:20:13,267 --> 00:20:17,055
Churchill called the fall of Singapore the worst disaster in British history.

394
00:20:18,200 --> 00:20:21,282
But America had a weapon Japan knew nothing about.

395
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:23,361
Codebreakers.

396
00:20:23,361 --> 00:20:26,762
Japan's secret messages were no longer secret.

397
00:20:27,900 --> 00:20:28,872
June 1942.

398
00:20:28,872 --> 00:20:33,151
Japan's plan was an ambush at Midway Island.

399
00:20:34,300 --> 00:20:37,226
But America was already waiting in the ambush.

400
00:20:38,367 --> 00:20:38,997
June 4.

401
00:20:38,997 --> 00:20:40,349
Just 5 minutes.

402
00:20:40,349 --> 00:20:47,196
In 5 minutes, American dive bombers set 3 Japanese aircraft carriers ablaze.

403
00:20:48,333 --> 00:20:50,449
The fourth carrier sank by evening.

404
00:20:51,567 --> 00:20:55,694
The backbone of the Japanese navy — broken in a single day.

405
00:20:56,833 --> 00:20:59,759
An empire won in 6 months began to shrink.

406
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:03,163
And so began island hopping.

407
00:21:03,163 --> 00:21:06,882
One island, one step at a time — toward Japan.

408
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:11,867
By the end of 1942: El Alamein.

409
00:21:11,867 --> 00:21:13,240
Stalingrad.

410
00:21:13,240 --> 00:21:14,113
Midway.

411
00:21:15,233 --> 00:21:16,536
3 continents.

412
00:21:16,536 --> 00:21:17,538
3 defeats.

413
00:21:17,538 --> 00:21:20,144
The Axis advance was over.

414
00:21:21,267 --> 00:21:23,394
Now the story changes.

415
00:21:23,394 --> 00:21:25,812
From defense — to attack.

416
00:21:26,933 --> 00:21:28,147
Chapter eleven.

417
00:21:28,147 --> 00:21:29,441
The last gamble.

418
00:21:30,567 --> 00:21:34,433
After Stalingrad, Hitler needed a great victory.

419
00:21:34,433 --> 00:21:35,400
At any cost.

420
00:21:36,533 --> 00:21:38,874
The place he chose was Kursk.

421
00:21:38,874 --> 00:21:41,052
A bulge in the Soviet line.

422
00:21:42,200 --> 00:21:43,135
July 1943.

423
00:21:43,135 --> 00:21:46,406
The largest tank battle in history.

424
00:21:47,533 --> 00:21:49,427
Nearly 6,000 tanks.

425
00:21:49,427 --> 00:21:51,321
2 million soldiers.

426
00:21:52,467 --> 00:21:55,549
But Soviet spies already knew the entire plan.

427
00:21:56,667 --> 00:22:02,492
They built a fortress of defenses — minefields, trenches, walls of artillery.

428
00:22:03,633 --> 00:22:06,663
The German attack hit that wall — and shattered.

429
00:22:07,800 --> 00:22:11,943
After Kursk, Germany never attacked in the east again.

430
00:22:11,943 --> 00:22:12,633
Not once.

431
00:22:13,767 --> 00:22:17,006
That same month, the Allies landed in Sicily.

432
00:22:18,133 --> 00:22:19,252
Italy shook.

433
00:22:19,252 --> 00:22:25,500
Mussolini — dictator for 20 years — was arrested by his own people.

434
00:22:26,633 --> 00:22:28,044
Italy switched sides.

435
00:22:29,167 --> 00:22:31,936
The Axis of 3 — now down to 2.

436
00:22:33,067 --> 00:22:35,343
Hitler was retreating on every front.

437
00:22:35,343 --> 00:22:37,743
But the biggest blow was still to come.

438
00:22:38,867 --> 00:22:40,591
On the beaches of France.

439
00:22:41,733 --> 00:22:43,357
Chapter twelve.

440
00:22:43,357 --> 00:22:44,006
D-Day.

441
00:22:45,133 --> 00:22:50,584
By 1944, the question was no longer whether the Allies would land in France.

442
00:22:50,584 --> 00:22:53,022
The question was where — and when.

443
00:22:54,167 --> 00:22:57,507
Hitler had turned the entire coast into a fortress.

444
00:22:57,507 --> 00:22:58,686
The Atlantic Wall.

445
00:22:59,833 --> 00:23:02,864
But the Allies had a weapon of their own.

446
00:23:02,864 --> 00:23:03,307
A lie.

447
00:23:04,433 --> 00:23:06,426
Operation Fortitude.

448
00:23:06,426 --> 00:23:08,418
An entire fake army.

449
00:23:08,418 --> 00:23:09,713
Rubber tanks.

450
00:23:09,713 --> 00:23:11,904
Phantom radio traffic.

451
00:23:13,033 --> 00:23:16,828
Germany was convinced the invasion would come at Calais.

452
00:23:16,828 --> 00:23:17,709
Not Normandy.

453
00:23:18,833 --> 00:23:20,379
June 6, 1944.

454
00:23:20,379 --> 00:23:23,352
The darkness before dawn.

455
00:23:24,500 --> 00:23:29,245
The largest seaborne invasion in history.

456
00:23:29,245 --> 00:23:30,634
7,000 ships.

457
00:23:30,634 --> 00:23:32,601
156,000 soldiers.

458
00:23:32,601 --> 00:23:34,453
In a single day.

459
00:23:35,600 --> 00:23:40,772
Americans, British, Canadians — across 5 beaches at once.

460
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:42,945
Utah.

461
00:23:42,945 --> 00:23:44,199
Omaha.

462
00:23:44,199 --> 00:23:45,243
Gold.

463
00:23:45,243 --> 00:23:46,288
Juno.

464
00:23:46,288 --> 00:23:47,542
Sword.

465
00:23:48,667 --> 00:23:50,721
Omaha was a slaughterhouse.

466
00:23:50,721 --> 00:23:53,918
Open sand, straight into the machine guns.

467
00:23:55,067 --> 00:23:58,776
In some first-wave companies, more than half the men went down.

468
00:23:59,900 --> 00:24:03,374
But by nightfall, all 5 beaches were in Allied hands.

469
00:24:04,500 --> 00:24:05,300
And Hitler?

470
00:24:05,300 --> 00:24:06,681
He slept for hours.

471
00:24:06,681 --> 00:24:10,534
His generals begged for permission to send the tanks.

472
00:24:11,667 --> 00:24:13,187
The lie kept working.

473
00:24:13,187 --> 00:24:17,388
For weeks, Germany waited for the real invasion at Calais.

474
00:24:18,533 --> 00:24:20,727
The Allied armies broke out of Normandy.

475
00:24:21,867 --> 00:24:24,714
August 25 — Paris liberated.

476
00:24:25,833 --> 00:24:28,458
4 years of occupation, over.

477
00:24:28,458 --> 00:24:32,677
Celebration, tears, and flags in the streets.

478
00:24:33,800 --> 00:24:37,131
Now Germany was caught between two millstones.

479
00:24:37,131 --> 00:24:38,942
The Allies from the west.

480
00:24:38,942 --> 00:24:41,114
The Soviet army from the east.

481
00:24:42,233 --> 00:24:45,452
And both were headed for the same destination.

482
00:24:45,452 --> 00:24:45,942
Berlin.

483
00:24:47,067 --> 00:24:48,488
Chapter thirteen.

484
00:24:48,488 --> 00:24:49,575
The collapse.

485
00:24:50,700 --> 00:24:54,466
In the east, Soviet vengeance rolled toward Germany.

486
00:24:54,466 --> 00:24:55,914
Operation Bagration.

487
00:24:55,914 --> 00:24:59,608
An entire German army group was wiped from the map.

488
00:25:00,733 --> 00:25:04,207
In the west, the Allied armies pushed toward the Rhine.

489
00:25:05,333 --> 00:25:06,485
December 1944.

490
00:25:06,485 --> 00:25:08,211
Hitler's last gamble.

491
00:25:08,211 --> 00:25:12,569
A surprise counterattack through the frozen Ardennes.

492
00:25:13,700 --> 00:25:15,398
The Battle of the Bulge.

493
00:25:15,398 --> 00:25:18,298
The bloodiest American battle of the war.

494
00:25:19,433 --> 00:25:22,328
After the first shock, the gamble collapsed.

495
00:25:22,328 --> 00:25:24,762
There was no fuel left for the tanks.

496
00:25:25,900 --> 00:25:29,153
Germany had nothing left.

497
00:25:29,153 --> 00:25:30,714
No soldiers.

498
00:25:30,714 --> 00:25:31,625
No oil.

499
00:25:31,625 --> 00:25:32,666
No time.

500
00:25:33,800 --> 00:25:36,073
Old men and children were sent to the front.

501
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:39,708
City after city was ground into rubble.

502
00:25:40,833 --> 00:25:41,912
April 1945.

503
00:25:41,912 --> 00:25:46,324
The Soviet army stood at the gates of Berlin.

504
00:25:46,324 --> 00:25:48,382
2.5 million soldiers.

505
00:25:49,500 --> 00:25:50,927
Chapter fourteen.

506
00:25:50,927 --> 00:25:51,851
The bunker.

507
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:54,996
Hitler was underground now.

508
00:25:54,996 --> 00:25:56,474
In the Führerbunker.

509
00:25:57,600 --> 00:26:01,962
On his maps, he was issuing orders to armies that no longer existed.

510
00:26:03,100 --> 00:26:05,643
April 20 — his 56th birthday.

511
00:26:05,643 --> 00:26:09,239
Above him, shells were falling on Berlin.

512
00:26:10,367 --> 00:26:11,732
April 30, 1945.

513
00:26:11,732 --> 00:26:16,192
With Soviet soldiers a few hundred meters away...

514
00:26:17,333 --> 00:26:18,900
Hitler shot himself.

515
00:26:20,033 --> 00:26:25,426
The man who started a war of tens of millions of deaths — gone.

516
00:26:25,426 --> 00:26:26,197
No trial.

517
00:26:26,197 --> 00:26:27,138
No answers.

518
00:26:28,267 --> 00:26:32,133
Two days later, the red flag flew over the roof of the Reichstag.

519
00:26:33,267 --> 00:26:34,430
May 8, 1945.

520
00:26:34,430 --> 00:26:37,727
Germany's unconditional surrender.

521
00:26:37,727 --> 00:26:40,346
The war in Europe was over.

522
00:26:41,467 --> 00:26:46,326
London, Paris, Moscow, New York — the streets erupted.

523
00:26:47,467 --> 00:26:50,628
But out in the Pacific, the war was still alive.

524
00:26:51,767 --> 00:26:52,948
Chapter fifteen.

525
00:26:52,948 --> 00:26:55,163
A light brighter than the sun.

526
00:26:56,300 --> 00:26:57,583
Japan was losing.

527
00:26:57,583 --> 00:26:59,696
But it was not surrendering.

528
00:27:00,833 --> 00:27:01,622
Iwo Jima.

529
00:27:01,622 --> 00:27:02,322
Okinawa.

530
00:27:02,322 --> 00:27:06,789
On every island, the fighting went to the last man.

531
00:27:07,933 --> 00:27:13,732
American planners estimated an invasion of Japan itself would cost hundreds of thousands more lives.

532
00:27:14,867 --> 00:27:19,863
And just then, in the American desert, a secret test lit the sky.

533
00:27:19,863 --> 00:27:21,554
The Manhattan Project.

534
00:27:22,700 --> 00:27:25,051
Mankind had learned to split the atom.

535
00:27:26,200 --> 00:27:30,104
August 6, 1945, 8:15 in the morning.

536
00:27:30,104 --> 00:27:31,189
Hiroshima.

537
00:27:32,333 --> 00:27:33,244
One bomb.

538
00:27:33,244 --> 00:27:34,154
One city.

539
00:27:34,154 --> 00:27:38,707
By the end of the year — around 140,000 dead.

540
00:27:39,833 --> 00:27:42,132
Three days later — Nagasaki.

541
00:27:43,267 --> 00:27:43,995
August 15.

542
00:27:43,995 --> 00:27:48,950
For the first time ever, the Emperor's voice was heard on the radio.

543
00:27:48,950 --> 00:27:50,189
The war was over.

544
00:27:51,333 --> 00:27:52,822
September 2, 1945.

545
00:27:52,822 --> 00:27:56,380
Signatures on the deck of the USS Missouri.

546
00:27:56,380 --> 00:27:59,771
The Second World War was officially over.

547
00:28:00,900 --> 00:28:01,806
6 years.

548
00:28:01,806 --> 00:28:04,296
60 to 80 million dead.

549
00:28:05,433 --> 00:28:08,630
And one question, argued over to this day.

550
00:28:08,630 --> 00:28:10,684
Did the bombs have to fall?

551
00:28:11,833 --> 00:28:13,730
History gives no answer.

552
00:28:13,730 --> 00:28:14,915
Only a warning.

553
00:28:16,033 --> 00:28:17,180
Chapter sixteen.

554
00:28:17,180 --> 00:28:19,115
A new world from the ashes.

555
00:28:20,233 --> 00:28:22,656
This war did not just redraw the map.

556
00:28:22,656 --> 00:28:25,144
It rebuilt the structure of the world.

557
00:28:26,267 --> 00:28:28,853
Europe's old great powers were bankrupt.

558
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:33,435
Two new superpowers now stood over everything.

559
00:28:33,435 --> 00:28:34,780
The United States.

560
00:28:34,780 --> 00:28:36,348
And the Soviet Union.

561
00:28:37,467 --> 00:28:39,512
The friendship soured fast.

562
00:28:39,512 --> 00:28:41,255
The Cold War had begun.

563
00:28:42,400 --> 00:28:46,449
Never again — in that hope, the United Nations was born.

564
00:28:47,567 --> 00:28:52,896
At Nuremberg, for the first time, leaders stood trial for crimes against humanity.

565
00:28:54,033 --> 00:28:55,187
And the empires?

566
00:28:55,187 --> 00:28:57,350
Their grip was broken forever.

567
00:28:58,500 --> 00:29:02,366
The colonies that had fought this war now demanded the bill be paid.

568
00:29:03,500 --> 00:29:05,015
1947 — India.

569
00:29:05,015 --> 00:29:09,795
Then, one by one, all of Asia and Africa.

570
00:29:10,933 --> 00:29:15,557
A war fought to save empires — ended up destroying them.

571
00:29:16,700 --> 00:29:18,050
Final chapter.

572
00:29:18,050 --> 00:29:19,208
The lessons.

573
00:29:20,333 --> 00:29:22,527
So what does the Second World War teach us?

574
00:29:23,667 --> 00:29:27,220
That hatred grows in the soil of humiliation and poverty.

575
00:29:28,367 --> 00:29:31,606
That when hatred finds a leader, it becomes policy.

576
00:29:32,733 --> 00:29:35,580
That ignoring a danger only makes it grow.

577
00:29:36,700 --> 00:29:40,171
And this — that starting a war is easy.

578
00:29:40,171 --> 00:29:41,951
Stopping one is not.

579
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:44,667
60 million people.

580
00:29:44,667 --> 00:29:46,757
Every number was a name.

581
00:29:46,757 --> 00:29:47,541
A family.

582
00:29:48,667 --> 00:29:51,724
They say history does not repeat itself.

583
00:29:51,724 --> 00:29:52,794
But it rhymes.

584
00:29:53,933 --> 00:29:55,759
That was the whole story.

585
00:29:55,759 --> 00:30:00,725
Six years that rewrote the world — and a warning that never expires.

586
00:30:01,867 --> 00:30:05,547
If this story stayed with you, remember why it was told.

587
00:30:05,547 --> 00:30:10,017
Not to relive the war — but to recognize the road that leads to one.

588
00:30:11,133 --> 00:30:16,499
There are more stories like this — complete, from the first moment to the last.

589
00:30:16,499 --> 00:30:19,283
To hear them all, subscribe to mindwired.
